DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  three  functions  hhccrreeaattee(),  hhsseeaarrcchh(),  and hhddeessttrrooyy() allow the
       caller to create and manage a hash search table containing entries con‐
       sisting of a key (a string) and associated  data.   Using  these  func‐
       tions, only one hash table can be used at a time.

       The  three  functions  hhccrreeaattee__rr(), hhsseeaarrcchh__rr(), hhddeessttrrooyy__rr() are reen‐
       trant versions that allow a program to use more than  one  hash  search
       table at the same time.  The last argument, _h_t_a_b, points to a structure
       that describes the table on which the function is to operate.  The pro‐
       grammer  should treat this structure as opaque (i.e., do not attempt to
       directly access or modify the fields in this structure).

       First a hash table must be created using hhccrreeaattee().  The  argument  _n_e_l
       specifies  the  maximum  number of entries in the table.  (This maximum
       cannot be changed later, so choose it wisely.)  The implementation  may
       adjust  this  value  upward to improve the performance of the resulting
       hash table.

       The hhccrreeaattee__rr() function performs the same task as hhccrreeaattee(),  but  for
       the  table  described by the structure _*_h_t_a_b.  The structure pointed to
       by _h_t_a_b must be zeroed before the first call to hhccrreeaattee__rr().

       The function hhddeessttrrooyy() frees the memory occupied  by  the  hash  table
       that  was  created  by hhccrreeaattee().  After calling hhddeessttrrooyy(), a new hash
       table can be created using hhccrreeaattee().  The hhddeessttrrooyy__rr()  function  per‐
       forms the analogous task for a hash table described by _*_h_t_a_b, which was
       previously created using hhccrreeaattee__rr().

       The  hhsseeaarrcchh()  function  searches  the hash table for an item with the
       same key as _i_t_e_m (where "the same" is determined using ssttrrccmmpp(3)),  and
       if successful returns a pointer to it.

       The  argument  _i_t_e_m is of type _E_N_T_R_Y, which is defined in _<_s_e_a_r_c_h_._h_> as
       follows:

           typedef struct entry {
               char *key;
               void *data;
           } ENTRY;

       The field _k_e_y points to a null‐terminated string which  is  the  search
       key.  The field _d_a_t_a points to data that is associated with that key.

       The  argument _a_c_t_i_o_n determines what hhsseeaarrcchh() does after an unsuccess‐
       ful search.  This argument must either have the  value  EENNTTEERR,  meaning
       insert a copy of _i_t_e_m (and return a pointer to the new hash table entry
       as the function result), or the value FFIINNDD, meaning that NULL should be
       returned.  (If _a_c_t_i_o_n is FFIINNDD, then _d_a_t_a is ignored.)

       The hhsseeaarrcchh__rr() function is like hhsseeaarrcchh() but operates on the hash ta‐
       ble   described  by  _*_h_t_a_b.   The  hhsseeaarrcchh__rr()  function  differs  from
       hhsseeaarrcchh() in that a pointer to the found item is returned  in  _*_r_e_t_v_a_l,
       rather than as the function result.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       hhccrreeaattee()  and hhccrreeaattee__rr() return nonzero on success.  They return 0 on
       error, with _e_r_r_n_o set to indicate the error.

       On success, hhsseeaarrcchh() returns a pointer to an entry in the hash  table.
       hhsseeaarrcchh()  returns  NULL  on error, that is, if _a_c_t_i_o_n is EENNTTEERR and the
       hash table is full, or _a_c_t_i_o_n is FFIINNDD and _i_t_e_m cannot be found  in  the
       hash  table.   hhsseeaarrcchh__rr()  returns nonzero on success, and 0 on error.
       In the event of an error, these two functions set _e_r_r_n_o to indicate the
       error.

EERRRROORRSS
       hhccrreeaattee__rr() and hhddeessttrrooyy__rr() can fail for the following reasons:

       EEIINNVVAALL _h_t_a_b is NULL.

       hhsseeaarrcchh() and hhsseeaarrcchh__rr() can fail for the following reasons:

       EENNOOMMEEMM _a_c_t_i_o_n was EENNTTEERR, _k_e_y was not found in the table, and there  was
